{"file_path":"c:\Users\emsen\research\library\domains\humanities\domains\sociology\domains\domesticity\_index.md","content":"# Domesticity

Domesticity names the practices, labor, and knowledge systems that sustain life within and around the household. It encompasses food production, shelter maintenance, care work, and the material reproduction of daily life — activities that political economy systematically undervalues despite their foundational role in every other form of production.
"}

{"file_path":"c:\Users\emsen\research\library\domains\humanities\domains\sociology\domains\domesticity\domains\gardening\_index.md","content":"# Gardening

Gardening is the practice of cultivating plants within a managed space for food, medicine, beauty, or ecological function. Unlike industrial agriculture, gardening operates at a scale where the cultivator maintains direct relationship with individual plants and soil conditions. It is simultaneously a technical discipline (requiring knowledge of botany, soil science, and local climate), a domestic practice (embedded in household labor and seasonal rhythm), and a cultural practice (carrying seed-saving traditions, indigenous polycultures, and community knowledge across generations).
"}

