{"file_path":"c:\Users\emsen\research\library\domains\science\domains\biology\domains\ecology\terms\biological-control.md","old_string":"The psyllid feeds on knotweed sap and causes leaf curling and stunted growth, reducing the plant's photosynthetic capacity and competitive vigor. Initial field results were disappointing — the psyllid struggled to establish persistent populations in the UK's cool, wet climate (the original collection was from southern Japan). Subsequent releases using psyllid strains from regions of Japan with climates more similar to the UK have shown improved survival, though measurable impact on knotweed populations has not yet been demonstrated at scale. The program continues, reflecting the reality that biocontrol operates on timescales of decades, not years.","new_string":"The psyllid feeds on knotweed sap and causes leaf curling and stunted growth, reducing the plant's photosynthetic capacity and competitive vigor. Initial field releases in 2011 were disappointing — the psyllid struggled to establish persistent populations due to predation by native insects and poor survival on older foliage. A second strain (the Murakami line, from a climate more similar to the UK's) was approved in 2020–2021 and has shown improved survival. Meanwhile, the program has expanded internationally: Canada released psyllids in 2014, the US approved releases in 2020, and Pennsylvania conducted its first operational release in summer 2025 using a strain selected specifically for Bohemian knotweed control.

A second biocontrol approach is under development: the leaf-spot fungus *Mycosphaerella polygoni-cuspidati*, a pathogen from knotweed's native range. Field trials at CABI UK (2019–2021) showed it could cause necrotic leaf spots and reduce growth. It has not been approved as a classical biocontrol agent due to potential non-target effects, but is being repositioned as a **mycoherbicide** — a biological product applied like a herbicide rather than released as a self-sustaining population.

The program reflects the reality that biocontrol operates on timescales of decades, not years.","replace_all":false}

{"file_path":"c:\Users\emsen\research\library\domains\science\domains\medicine\domains\traditional-chinese-medicine\terms\blood-stasis.md","old_string":"Research programs in China, Japan, and integrative medicine centers have established statistically significant correlations between TCM blood stasis diagnosis and these biomedical markers [@chen2004]. This does not mean blood stasis "is" microcirculatory dysfunction — the concepts operate at different levels of description — but the convergence validates that TCM's clinical observation identified a real pathological phenomenon through functional assessment that biomedicine later described through molecular mechanisms.","new_string":"Recent research has identified a particularly striking structural correlate: **fibrinaloid microclots** — anomalous, dense clots resistant to fibrinolysis that block microcapillaries and reduce oxygen transfer. These microclots occur in chronic inflammatory and vascular diseases and are now understood to be mechanistically responsible for many of the symptoms TCM has attributed to blood stasis for centuries. The parallel with Virchow's triad (vessel wall damage, blood flow stasis, hypercoagulable state) is direct.

Research programs in China, Japan, and integrative medicine centers have established statistically significant correlations between TCM blood stasis diagnosis and these biomedical markers [@chen2004]. The classical formula **Xue Fu Zhu Yu Tang** (血府逐瘀汤, "Drive Out Stasis from the Mansion of Blood"), developed by Wang Qing-Ren in the 1830s, has been the subject of randomized controlled trials and is available as an FDA-approved capsule in China (since 2002) for coronary heart disease and hyperlipidemia — a rare example of a traditional formula validated through modern clinical trial methodology.

This convergence does not mean blood stasis "is" microcirculatory dysfunction — the concepts operate at different levels of description — but it validates that TCM's clinical observation identified a real pathological phenomenon through functional assessment that biomedicine later described through molecular mechanisms.","replace_all":false}
